            <div xml:id="d450">

                <pb facs="0744" n="618" sameAs="frus:frus1925v01/pg618"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/6791: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-11-28T18:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">November 28,
                                1925—6 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 7:49 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>502. 1. Report in first paragraph my 497 November 25, 5 p.m. partially erroneous.
                    Latest information is that while Yang Yu-ting <pb facs="0745" n="619"
                        xml:id="pg619"/> has fled to Dairen [and] serious defection among Fengtien
                    forces south of the Wall amounted to factional fighting, there has been no <hi
                        rendition="#italic">coup d’état</hi> at Mukden and Chang Tso-lin is still in
                    control there. Although the situation is still undefined it would seem that
                    General Kuo Sung-ling and the Manchurian forces between Tientsin and the Wall,
                    having come to an agreement with Marshal Feng Yu-hsiang, attempted to march on
                    Mukden with the purpose of ousting Marshal Chang, which attempt was blocked by
                    loyal Manchurian forces at Shanhaikwan.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
